ERR1 = "Provide a handler for Program_Error since every alternative of this selective accept can become closed"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.2.2",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.2": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.2.2 Defensive Task Communication",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.2": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.2": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Provide a handler for exception Program_Error whenever you cannot
avoid a selective accept statement whose alternatives can all be closed
(Honeywell 1986).</p>
<p>&#8226; Make systematic use of handlers for Tasking_Error.</p>
<p>&#8226; Be prepared to handle exceptions during a rendezvous.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider using a when others exception handler.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>This block allows recovery from exceptions raised while attempting to
communicate a command to another task:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">Accelerate:
   begin
      Throttle.Increase(Step);
    exception
       when Tasking_Error     =>          ...
       when Constraint_Error =>           ...
       when Throttle_Too_Wide =>          ...
       ...
    end Accelerate;
</code></pre>
<p>In this select statement, if all the guards happen to be closed, the
program can continue by executing the else part. There is no need for a
handler for Program_Error. Other exceptions can still be raised while
evaluating the guards or attempting to communicate. You will also need to
include an exception handler in the task Throttle so that it can continue
to execute after an exception is raised during the rendezvous:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">...
Guarded:
    begin
       select
          when Condition_1 =>
              accept Entry_1;
       or
            when Condition_2 =>
               accept Entry_2;
       else -- all alternatives closed
          ...
       end select;
    exception
       when Constraint_Error =>
          ...
    end Guarded;
</code></pre>
<p>In this select statement, if all the guards happen to be closed, exception
Program_Error will be raised. Other exceptions can still be raised while
evaluating the guards or attempting to communicate:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">Guarded:
   begin
      select
         when Condition_1 =>
             accept Entry_1;
         or
            when Condition_2 =>
               delay Fraction_Of_A_Second;
         end select;
      exception
         when Program_Error       =>   ...
         when Constraint_Error    =>   ...
      end Guarded;
...
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>The exception Program_Error is raised if a selective accept statement
(select statement containing accepts) is reached, all of whose
alternatives are closed (i.e., the guards evaluate to False and there are
no alternatives without guards), unless there is an else part. When all
alternatives are closed, the task can never again progress, so there is
by definition an error in its programming. You must be prepared to handle
this error should it occur.</p>
<p>Because an else part cannot have a guard, it can never be closed off as
an alternative action; thus, its presence prevents Program_Error. However,
an else part, a delay alternative, and a terminate alternative are all
mutually exclusive, so you will not always be able to provide an else
part. In these cases, you must be prepared to handle Program_Error.</p>
<p>The exception Tasking_Error can be raised in the calling task whenever
it attempts to communicate. There are many situations permitting this.
Few of them are preventable by the calling task.</p>
<p>If an exception is raised during a rendezvous and not handled in the
accept statement, it is propagated to both tasks and must be handled in
two places (see Guideline 5.8).</p>
<p>The handling of the others exception can be used to avoid propagating
unexpected exceptions to callers (when this is the desired effect) and to
localize the logic for dealing with unexpected exceptions in the
rendezvous. After handling, an unknown exception should normally be
raised again because the final decision of how to deal with it might need
to be made at the outermost scope of the task body.</p>

<p><b>Notes</b></p>
<p>There are other ways to prevent Program_Error at a selective accept.
These involve leaving at least one alternative unguarded or proving that
at least one guard will evaluate True under all circumstances. The point
here is that you or your successors will make mistakes in trying to do
this, so you should prepare to handle the inevitable exception.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>The first bullet is automated. A selective accept statement is
identified structurally: it must contain at least one <code>accept</code>
alternative (distinguishing it from a timed or conditional entry call,
whose first alternative is instead an entry-call statement, and from an
asynchronous select, marked by <code>then abort</code>). If that statement has no
<code>else</code> part and every one of its alternatives carries a <code>when</code> guard
(no unguarded <code>accept</code>, <code>delay</code>, or <code>terminate</code> to fall back on), it can
raise <code>Program_Error</code>, and the enclosing task is checked for a handler
for <code>Program_Error</code> or <code>others</code> anywhere in its own body &#8212; matching the
same task-wide (not block-scoped) approach already used by
<code>ADA95_5.9.8</code> for Finalize/Adjust handlers, since Understand reports a
task's own exception handlers as a flat set regardless of which nested
block declares them. This means a handler present anywhere in the task,
even one that would not actually intercept this particular exception
before it propagates past a different nested block, is accepted as
satisfying the guideline; distinguishing that would require modeling
exception propagation through nested block scopes, which this check does
not attempt.</p>
<p>The remaining three bullets are not automated. "Make systematic use of
handlers for Tasking_Error" and "be prepared to handle exceptions during
a rendezvous" have no violation shape: Ada allows an exception raised at
any point to be legitimately handled by any enclosing scope up to the
task body itself, so the absence of a handler at any specific call site
or accept statement is not itself evidence of a defect. "Consider using a
when others exception handler" is a design-choice recommendation with no
corresponding violation.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OPENER_KEYWORDS = ("if", "case", "loop", "select", "begin", "do")
_ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS = ("accept", "delay", "terminate")
_HANDLED_EXCEPTION_NAMES = ("program_error", "others")


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _is_statement_start(lex):
    prev = lex.previous(True, True)

    return prev is None or prev.text() in (";", "then", "else", "loop", "begin", "do")


def _scan_alternative(lex):
    # A guard is skipped by scanning to its alternative keyword directly,
    # rather than hunting for its own "=>", since none of accept/delay/
    # terminate can appear as an identifier inside a guard's expression.
    guarded = False

    if lex and lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "when":
        guarded = True
        lex = _next_significant(lex)

        while lex and lex.text().lower() not in _ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS:
            lex = _next_significant(lex)

    alt_kind = lex.text().lower() if lex else None

    return alt_kind, guarded, _next_significant(lex) if lex else None


def _scan_select_alternatives(lex):
    # lex is positioned right after the statement's own "select" keyword.
    # A single depth counter is enough to skip nested if/case/loop/select/
    # begin/do constructs: Ada requires each to be properly nested and
    # closed by exactly one "end", so counting openers and "end" tokens
    # (without checking which closes which) safely finds this select's
    # own top-level "or"/"else"/"end select" among any of its own.
    alt_kind, guarded, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)
    alternatives = [(alt_kind, guarded)]
    has_else = False
    depth = 0

    while lex:
        text = lex.text().lower() if lex.token() == "Keyword" else None

        if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
            depth += 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if text == "end":
            if depth == 0:
                return alternatives, has_else

            depth -= 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "or":
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            alt_kind, guarded, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)
            alternatives.append((alt_kind, guarded))
            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "else":
            has_else = True

        lex = _next_significant(lex)

    return alternatives, has_else


def _find_select_statements(lexer, start_line, end_line):
    results = []
    lex = lexer.lexeme(start_line, 1)

    while lex and lex.line_begin() <= end_line:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "select"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            select_line, select_col = lex.line_begin(), lex.column_begin()
            alternatives, has_else = _scan_select_alternatives(
                _next_significant(lex))
            results.append((select_line, select_col, alternatives, has_else))

        lex = lex.next()

    return results


def _needs_program_error_handler(alternatives, has_else):
    is_selective_accept = any(kind == "accept" for kind, _ in alternatives)
    all_guarded = all(guarded for _, guarded in alternatives)

    return is_selective_accept and not has_else and all_guarded


def _task_ranges(file):
    ranges = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare Body", "Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        end_ref = ent.ref("Endby")

        if not end_ref:
            continue

        ranges.append((decl_ref.line(), end_ref.line(), ent))

    return ranges


def _task_handles_program_error(task_ent):
    return any(
        h.ent().name().lower() in _HANDLED_EXCEPTION_NAMES
        for h in task_ent.refs("Handle")
    )


def check(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

    for start, end, ent in _task_ranges(file):
        for select_line, select_col, alternatives, has_else in _find_select_statements(
            lexer, start, end
        ):
            if _needs_program_error_handler(
                alternatives, has_else
            ) and not _task_handles_program_error(ent):
                check.violation(ent, file, select_line, select_col, ERR1)
